<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Naveta des Tudons</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Naveta des Tudons is a prehistoric stone tomb near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ciutadella-de-menorca">Ciutadella</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menorca">Menorca</a>, shaped like an upturned boat. Built of large dressed stones without mortar in the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>, it was a collective burial chamber on two levels, in which the remains of dozens of people were found. It is the best-preserved monument of its kind in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balearic-islands">Balearic Islands</a> and a symbol of Menorca's prehistoric past.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/naveta-des-tudons.jpg" alt="Naveta des Tudons" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tudons01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Navetas, of which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menorca">Menorca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Majorca</a> hold several dozen dwelling and funerary complexes, belong to the pre-Talaiotic age, before the island's Talaiotic culture. The name, a diminutive of nau, a ship, was given by Dr Juan Ramis in his book Celtic antiques on the island of Menorca of 1818, after their likeness to upturned boats. The Naveta d'Es Tudons, named after its site, the woodpigeons, served as a collective ossuary between 1200 and 750 BCE. Radiocarbon dates from bones in Menorca's funerary navetas point to use between about 1130 and 820 BCE, though navetas of this kind are probably older.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The tomb stands about 3 miles from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ciutadella-de-menorca">Ciutadella</a>, beside the road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahon">Mahón</a>, on slightly rising ground in a sloping valley. It is 14.5 m long, 6.5 m wide and 4.55 m high, and was once about 6 m high; its front is a trapezoidal façade and its rear a rounded apse. The walls are horizontal corbelled courses of large limestone blocks, dressed with a hammer and laid on a foundation of still larger blocks set on edge.</p><p>A low doorway of 0.57 by 0.75 m, rebated for a closing slab, leads through a short passage and an antechamber to the lower chamber, 7.45 m long, roofed with great slabs. Above it lies a narrower upper chamber with a ceiling only 0.85 m high, pierced by holes, probably for ventilation. The disarticulated bones of the dead were stored in the lower chamber once the flesh was gone, while the upper one was probably used to dry fresh corpses.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Navetas were described early in the 19th century but excavated only in the 20th. This one, entered in the Spanish heritage register on 3 June 1931, was excavated and restored by the archaeologist Lluís Pericot García in 1959–1960, when one trepanned skull was found and the missing top courses were put back. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronze</a> bracelets and buttons of bone and pottery from it are shown in the Museu de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menorca">Menorca</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahon">Mahón</a>. A local tale tells of two giants who competed for a girl, one building the naveta while the other dug a well. Menorcans avoided the navetas until well into the 19th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Naveta d'Es Tudons” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It grew out of the earlier Naviform culture of boat-shaped houses and stone tombs such as the <b>Naveta des Tudons</b> on Menorca.</blockquote><a href="/state/balearic-islanders">Balearic Islanders</a></li></ul></section>
